LESSONS_S1 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Customer record: scope and structure", "11", "<p>This module covers the Customer master and the selling foundations in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: create Customer records with the full field set; design the Customer Group and Territory trees; maintain contacts and addresses including multi-site customers; operate credit limits and payment terms under the owner's ladder; run the customer onboarding gate; and run the customer data reads on their cadences.</p><p><b>Where the Customer record sits.</b> Every selling document — Quotation, Sales Order, Delivery Note, Sales Invoice — names a customer, and pulls its behaviour from that customer's record: the price list, the payment terms, the credit limit, the tax treatment, the addresses. A selling document created against a well-built Customer record pre-fills correctly; one created against a thin or wrong record requires per-document correction, and per-document correction is where pricing and terms errors enter.</p><p><b>What reads the Customer record.</b></p><p>1. <b>The selling documents</b> — every transaction in modules 3-7 begins by selecting the customer.<br>2. <b>The receivables ledger</b> — the Accounts track's customer balances, statements, and aging all key on the record.<br>3. <b>The pricing machinery</b> — module 2's price lists and pricing rules resolve through the customer's group and record.<br>4. <b>The credit controls</b> — Chapter 5's limit checks run per customer per company.<br>5. <b>The reports</b> — sales by territory, receivables by group, customer-wise profitability.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The sales function operates the records; the owner decides credit limits and payment terms per the ladder (Chapter 5); the accountant owns the receivables side and the tax fields' correctness; record creation runs through the onboarding gate (Chapter 6), by the roles the matrix permits.</p><p><b>Build order.</b> The trees before the records, the records before the documents:</p><p>1. The Customer Group tree designed (Chapter 3) — the commercial classification.<br>2. The Territory tree designed (Chapter 3) — the geography.<br>3. The onboarding gate written (Chapter 6) — the checklist and the roles.<br>4. Customers created through the gate, with contacts and addresses (Chapters 2, 4).<br>5. Credit and terms set per the ladder (Chapter 5).</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Customer doctype walk. Chapter 3: the group and territory trees. Chapter 4: contacts and addresses. Chapter 5: credit and terms. Chapter 6: the onboarding gate. Chapter 7: the customer data reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Customer doctype", "12", "<p>Open Customer (awesome bar) &gt; New. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Customer Name.</b> The legal or trading name per the naming convention: the registered name for companies, consistently formatted, without abbreviations that collide. The name prints on every document and statement; it is entered once, correctly, at the gate.</p><p><b>2. Customer Type.</b> Company or Individual — the classification that drives which tax and compliance fields apply, and how the record reads in reporting.</p><p><b>3. Customer Group.</b> The commercial classification from Chapter 3's tree — retail counters, chains, institutions, distributors. The group is a working field, not a label: default price lists and payment terms can derive from it, and the receivables and sales reports aggregate by it. A customer in the wrong group prices from the wrong list.</p><p><b>4. Territory.</b> The geography from Chapter 3's tree. Sales reporting, targets, and territory-based analyses key on it.</p><p><b>5. Currency and Default Price List.</b> The billing currency, and the price list the customer's documents pre-fill from (module 2 covers the lists). The resolution order at document time: the customer's own default, else the group's, else the selling default — the same decided-chain pattern as the stock defaults, and the same rule: the chain is wired once, by convention, and verified.</p><p><b>6. Payment Terms Template.</b> The default terms the customer's invoices carry (Chapter 5). Set from the ladder's decision, not typed ad hoc per invoice.</p><p><b>7. The credit limit section.</b> Per company: the credit limit amount, and the check behaviour (Chapter 5's machinery). The limit is entered here but decided at the ladder; the field carries the decision, and Chapter 7's changes read audits that it moved only by decision.</p><p><b>8. Tax fields.</b> The tax category where the site uses category-based tax rules, and the tax identification numbers per the compliance requirements — for Nigerian B2B customers, the TIN the e-invoicing and statutory reporting require. The accountant rules which fields are mandatory per customer type; the gate enforces them at creation, because a missing TIN discovered at invoicing blocks the document at the worst moment.</p><p><b>9. Disabled.</b> Customers are retired by the Disabled flag at zero balance, never deleted — document history references every customer that ever traded, per the standing retirement pattern.</p><p><b>Verification per record.</b> The name per convention; type correct; group and territory from the trees; price list and terms per the decisions; credit per the ladder; tax fields complete for the type; primary contact and address attached (Chapter 4).</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Customer Group and Territory trees", "12", "<p>Two trees classify every customer. This chapter is their design procedure.</p><p><b>1. What each tree answers.</b></p><p>1. <b>Customer Group</b> — the commercial question: what kind of buyer is this, and what pricing and terms behaviour follows? Groups drive price list and terms defaults, and the receivables and margin reports aggregate by them.<br>2. <b>Territory</b> — the geographic question: where is this customer, and which sales responsibility covers them? Territories drive sales reporting and targets.</p><p>The two are independent classifications: a chain customer in Ibadan and a retail counter in Ibadan share a territory and differ in group. Designing one tree to answer both questions produces a tree that answers neither cleanly.</p><p><b>2. The Customer Group tree design.</b> Open Customer Group Tree (awesome bar). The design procedure, on paper first per the standing pattern:</p><p>1. <b>List the commercial classes the business actually treats differently</b> — different price lists, different terms, different credit postures. For a pharmaceutical distributor: retail counters, chain pharmacies, hospitals and institutions, sub-distributors. A class with no behavioural difference is not a group; it is a report filter at most.<br>2. <b>Three levels or fewer</b>, per the standing tree rule: the root, the major classes, and subdivisions only where behaviour genuinely differs.<br>3. <b>Wire the defaults</b> — where a group's members share a price list or terms template, set it at the group so new members inherit; record the wiring in the convention.</p><p><b>3. The Territory tree design.</b> The same procedure for geography: the root, the regions the business manages against (Lagos, South-West, per the sales structure), and cities or zones only where reporting or responsibility requires them. The tree mirrors how sales responsibility is actually assigned — a territory no one owns is a reporting row without a use.</p><p><b>4. The tree change discipline.</b> Both trees change by decision, not per customer: a new group or territory is a structural decision recorded with its reason (what behaviour or responsibility it captures), because each new node fragments the history of the reports that aggregate on it. Reclassifying an existing customer is an ordinary edit with a note; restructuring a tree mid-year is a governed event with the report consumers told.</p><p><b>5. The classification read.</b> Quarterly: customers per group and territory, sampled against the definitions — the retail counter that grew into a chain and was never regrouped prices from the wrong list until this read finds it. The read closes with the corrections applied and the fragmenting-node check: groups or territories with a handful of members and no behavioural difference are merge candidates for the next structural review.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Contacts and addresses", "12", "<p>Contacts and addresses are separate records linked to the customer. This chapter is their structure and the multi-site pattern.</p><p><b>1. The Contact record.</b> One record per person: name, designation, phone, email, linked to the customer (a contact can link to several parties). Per customer:</p><p>1. <b>The primary contact</b> — the default the documents and communications pull.<br>2. <b>Role-specific contacts</b> — the buyer, the accounts payable contact for statements and collections (Chapter 5's statements go to a named person, not a company), the receiving contact per site where deliveries need one.</p><p><b>2. The Address record.</b> One record per physical address: lines, city, state, country, linked to the customer, with two flags:</p><p>1. <b>Preferred Billing Address</b> — where invoices and statements address.<br>2. <b>Preferred Shipping Address</b> — where deliveries default.</p><p>Documents pull the preferred addresses and allow per-document override — the delivery to a different site selects that site's address on the document, and the address prints on the delivery paperwork the driver carries.</p><p><b>3. The multi-site customer pattern.</b> A chain with many branches and one payer is one Customer record — the payer — with one billing address and many shipping addresses (one per branch, named by the branch: the chain's own branch codes in the address title keep the picker unambiguous). The consequences, stated:</p><p>1. <b>One receivables balance</b> — the chain owes as one account; statements, credit limit, and collections run against the payer, which is how the chain itself operates.<br>2. <b>Per-branch delivery</b> — each Delivery Note selects its branch's shipping address; the delivery history per branch remains readable by address.<br>3. <b>The alternative — child customers per branch — is used only where branches genuinely pay separately</b>, because separate Customer records mean separate balances, separate credit limits, and statements the chain's head office did not ask for. The pattern is decided at onboarding from one question: who pays?</p><p><b>4. Address and contact hygiene.</b> The details that age fastest in any register are people and addresses. The standing read, quarterly: primary contacts confirmed current for the top accounts (the collections function surfaces dead contacts naturally — a statement that bounces is a hygiene finding); shipping addresses sampled against recent delivery paperwork; leaver contacts at customer organisations replaced when known. Hygiene findings are ordinary edits with dates — the record's history shows when the business knew.</p><p><b>5. What does not go in contacts and addresses.</b> Terms, prices, and credit notes belong on the customer and its documents, not in contact remarks; delivery instructions that are permanent belong on the address record; per-order instructions belong on the order. The rule keeps each fact where the documents that need it will read it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Credit and terms", "13", "<p>Credit is the business lending money to its customers. This chapter is the machinery and the controls.</p><p><b>1. Payment Terms Templates.</b> Open Payment Terms Template (awesome bar) &gt; New: named templates — Cash, 30 Days, 45 Days, or split terms (a percentage on invoice, the balance at a due interval). Templates are the only lawful source of terms: the ladder decides which template a customer receives; the template sets due dates on every invoice automatically; and the receivables aging (the Accounts track) computes from those due dates. Terms typed per invoice bypass the decision and corrupt the aging.</p><p><b>2. The terms and credit ladder.</b> The written policy, owner-approved, that decides who may extend what:</p><p>1. <b>Cash terms</b> — the default for new customers below the threshold; no approval needed.<br>2. <b>Credit terms within standard bands</b> (30 or 45 days, limits per band) — approved by the sales manager per the ladder's figures, with the customer's trade references or history documented.<br>3. <b>Above-band terms or limits</b> — the owner's decision, documented.</p><p>Every credit decision files with the customer: the approved band, the approver, the date, the basis. Credit extended without its documented decision is Chapter 9's first error.</p><p><b>3. The credit limit machinery.</b> On the Customer record, per company: the limit amount and the enforcement behaviour. At document submission the system checks outstanding balance plus the new document against the limit:</p><p>1. <b>The block</b> — the standard posture: a Sales Order or Invoice that would exceed the limit does not submit.<br>2. <b>The bypass role</b> — the named role (per the matrix) that may submit over the limit; every bypass is a decision by that role, visible in the document trail, and the monthly over-limit read lists each one with its approver.<br>3. <b>Ignore-check settings</b> exist per customer for the rare standing exception — a register row with the owner's reason, not a convenience toggle.</p><p><b>4. Statements and the collections tie.</b> The Statement of Accounts per customer — the invoice, payment, and balance history — sends on the monthly cadence to the accounts payable contact (Chapter 4), and on demand at collection conversations. Statements are the customer's view of Chapter 5 working: terms on every invoice, due dates computed, payments allocated. The collections procedures themselves belong to module 7; this chapter's contribution is the data they run on.</p><p><b>5. The credit review.</b> Per the calendar: top exposures reviewed quarterly (limit against payment behaviour — the aging per customer from the Accounts track's reports); limits raised or reduced by the ladder with the decision filed; the annual full pass over all credit customers riding the receivables year-end. Credit that is never reviewed only moves in one direction — upward, one exception at a time — which is what the review exists to prevent.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The onboarding gate", "12", "<p>Customers enter the register by gate. This chapter is the checklist and its operation.</p><p><b>1. Why a gate.</b> Every field Chapter 2 walked is cheap to complete at creation and expensive to fix in flight: the wrong group prices documents until the classification read finds it; the missing TIN blocks invoicing at month-end; the undecided terms become whatever the first invoice typed. The gate is fifteen minutes that ends those cases.</p><p><b>2. The gate checklist.</b> In order:</p><p>1. <b>The duplicate check first</b> — the register searched by name fragments, phone, email, and TIN before creation. A duplicate customer splits one buyer's history and balance across records: credit checked against half the exposure, statements that disagree with the customer's own ledger, and a merge remediation later. One minute at the gate.<br>2. <b>The paying-entity question</b> — who pays? The answer decides the Chapter 4 pattern: one record with many shipping addresses, or separate customers. Asked and recorded at the gate, because restructuring after documents exist is a remediation.<br>3. <b>Classification</b> — group and territory from the trees, per the definitions.<br>4. <b>Terms and credit per the ladder</b> — cash by default; credit only with its documented decision (Chapter 5).<br>5. <b>Tax and compliance fields</b> — TIN and the mandatory fields per customer type, verified against the customer's documents, because compliance fields transcribed from a phone call are the invoicing blocks of the following month.<br>6. <b>Contacts and addresses</b> — the primary pair at minimum; the accounts payable contact for credit customers; the shipping set for multi-site customers.<br>7. <b>The first-document check</b> — a draft quotation against the new record, confirming the price list, terms, and addresses pre-fill as intended; the draft deleted.</p><p><b>3. Who operates the gate.</b> Customer creation is permitted to the roles the matrix names — the sales function's designated users — with the credit fields writable per the ladder's roles only. Bulk imports (a customer book migration) run as governed imports against the same checklist columns, sampled after load per the import procedure.</p><p><b>4. The gate's monthly read.</b> Customers created in the month sampled against the checklist: fields complete, decisions filed, duplicates absent. A gate that is not read drifts into a form-filling exercise; the monthly sample is what keeps the fifteen minutes honest, and its findings feed the checklist's next revision.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The customer data reads", "12", "<p>The standing reads that keep the customer register true, each with its cadence and action.</p><p><b>1. The duplicate-candidate read — monthly.</b> Near-identical names, shared phones or TINs across records, flagged for review. Confirmed duplicates merge per the remediation procedure (documents re-pointed per the data-fix rules, one surviving record); near-misses are noted so the next gate search finds them. The read is cheap while the register is young and priceless once collections depend on whole balances.</p><p><b>2. The classification read — quarterly (Chapter 3).</b> Group and territory sampled against the definitions; regrades applied; fragmenting nodes flagged for the structural review.</p><p><b>3. The credit changes read — monthly.</b> Every change to a credit limit or terms template in the month, with who changed it, listed against the ladder's filed decisions. The read is the audit that the fields move only by decision: a limit that moved without its paper is corrected and the permission gap closed. The over-limit bypass list (Chapter 5) files alongside it — the two together are the credit control's monthly evidence.</p><p><b>4. The dormancy read — quarterly.</b> Customers with no documents across the window, split by class: credit customers with open balances and no activity route to collections (module 7); cash customers with no activity for the policy's period are candidates for the disabled flag at the annual pass. Dormant records with live credit limits are the specific finding — an unused limit is exposure with no relationship watching it, and the read reduces it by decision.</p><p><b>5. The hygiene read — quarterly (Chapter 4).</b> Contacts and addresses for the top accounts confirmed current; bounce findings from statements actioned.</p><p><b>6. The receivables tie.</b> The customer register's ultimate read belongs to the Accounts track: the receivables aging per customer, monthly, with the statements cycle. This module's registers exist so that read is trustworthy — one record per payer, terms from templates, limits by decision. Where the aging surfaces a register problem (a balance split across duplicates, terms that disagree with the filed decision), the finding routes back to this module's remediation lanes, and the correction is made in the register, not worked around in the report.</p><p><b>7. The reads' filing.</b> Each read's output — the list, the corrections, the date — files with the sales function's records per the one-lookup standard, and the quarterly reads ride the same calendar as the stock and access recertifications so the review rhythm is one habit, not five.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: HealthTrade onboards the chain", "13", "<p>This chapter applies Chapters 2-7 to a real onboarding: a chain pharmacy group approaching HealthTrade for supply. Configuration values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>The prospect.</b> A chain pharmacy group with <b>30 branches — 24 in Lagos, 6 in Ibadan</b> — requests a supply relationship: central purchasing, head-office payment, deliveries to branches.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the paying-entity question (Chapters 4, 6).</b> Asked first at the gate: who pays? The answer — head office pays for all branches — decides the pattern: <b>one Customer record</b> (the group's registered company), one receivables balance, one credit limit, with per-branch shipping addresses. The alternative (30 child customers) is recorded as considered and rejected with the reason: the chain pays as one entity, and 30 balances would manufacture reconciliation work both sides.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the record built (Chapter 2).</b> Customer Type: Company; Customer Group: <b>Chain Pharmacies</b> (the group whose defaults carry the trade price list); Territory: the group's head-office territory, with sales reporting noting the two-city footprint. Tax fields: the TIN verified against the chain's registration documents at the gate. The duplicate check runs first and returns clean.</p><p><b>Step 3 — addresses and contacts (Chapter 4).</b> One billing address (head office); <b>30 shipping addresses</b>, titled with the chain's own branch codes so document pickers are unambiguous. Contacts: the head-office buyer (primary), the accounts payable contact (statements), and the two regional operations contacts for delivery coordination.</p><p><b>Step 4 — terms and credit (Chapter 5).</b> The chain requests credit terms. Per the ladder: the requested volume places the decision above the standard bands, so it goes to the owner with the trade references and the opening-volume projection. The decision, filed with the record: <b>45-day terms</b> on the named template, an opening credit limit per the projection, review scheduled at ninety days. The limit enters the record's credit section with the block posture standard; the bypass role confirmed as the named holders only.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the first-document check and the first cycle (Chapters 6-7).</b> The draft quotation confirms pre-fill: trade price list from the group, 45-day terms from the template, head-office billing, branch shipping selectable. The first month's documents flow: orders from head office, deliveries per branch address, invoices to the payer, the first statement to the named accounts payable contact. The ninety-day credit review runs on schedule against actual payment behaviour, and the limit decision is re-filed with the review's data — the ladder operating as procedure rather than exception.</p><p><b>The register evidence.</b> The gate file holds the duplicate check, the paying-entity decision, the classification, the credit decision and review, and the first-document check — the onboarding answerable from one folder, per the standing standard.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in customer foundations, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — credit without a decision.</b> Symptom: terms typed per invoice; limits set in conversation; the aging computed from improvised due dates. Correction: templates applied, the ladder's decisions filed retroactively where defensible, limits reviewed. Prevention: terms only from templates, credit only from the ladder, and the monthly changes read.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the duplicate customer.</b> Symptom: one buyer's history and balance split across records; credit checked against partial exposure; statements the customer disputes. Correction: the merge remediation with documents re-pointed. Prevention: the gate's duplicate check first, and the monthly candidate read.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the wrong multi-site pattern.</b> Symptom: 30 child customers for one payer, or one record for branches that pay separately; balances that match nobody's ledger. Correction: the restructure remediation per the data-fix rules. Prevention: the paying-entity question asked and recorded at the gate.</p><p><b>Error 4 — classification drift.</b> Symptom: customers priced from the wrong list because their group no longer matches their behaviour; territory reports that mislead. Correction: regrades from the classification read. Prevention: the quarterly read against the definitions and the tree change discipline.</p><p><b>Error 5 — compliance fields at invoicing time.</b> Symptom: missing TINs discovered when the invoice will not submit; month-end blocked on data entry. Correction: the fields completed from the customer's documents. Prevention: the gate's mandatory-fields step, verified against documents at creation.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the unwatched limit.</b> Symptom: dormant customers with live limits; credit that only ever ratchets upward; bypasses nobody lists. Correction: the dormancy and over-limit reads run, limits reduced by decision. Prevention: the quarterly credit review and the monthly bypass list.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the Customer doctype walk including the resolution chains, the credit section, and the retirement rule; design both trees with the behavioural-difference test and the change discipline; structure contacts and addresses including the multi-site pattern from the paying-entity question; operate templates, the ladder, the limit machinery with its bypass controls, statements, and the credit review; run the onboarding gate end to end; and run the six data reads on their cadences.</p><p><b>Module rule 1: every selling document stands on its Customer record — created through the gate with the duplicate check and the paying-entity question, classified by the trees, carrying terms only from templates and credit only from the ladder's filed decisions, and kept true by the scheduled reads.</b> Module 2 covers items and price lists.</p>"),
]

